WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.638
Choosing a local vision model is an engineering decision, not a popularity contest.

00:00:05.638 --> 00:00:09.899
Vision-language models differ across OCR, objects, counting, charts, screenshots, descriptions,

00:00:09.899 --> 00:00:11.603
and visual question answering.

00:00:11.603 --> 00:00:14.585
The use case defines which differences matter.

00:00:14.585 --> 00:00:19.698
Hardware fit includes VRAM or unified memory, system RAM, accelerator support, concurrency,

00:00:19.698 --> 00:00:20.976
and operational headroom.

00:00:20.976 --> 00:00:24.384
Model size, quantization, image-token budget, runtime, prompt template,

00:00:24.384 --> 00:00:27.366
and thinking mode form one tested configuration.

00:00:27.366 --> 00:00:31.627
Accuracy must be considered with completion, latency, memory failures, hallucinations,

00:00:31.627 --> 00:00:35.461
OCR and counting errors, and consistency across repeated runs.

00:00:35.461 --> 00:00:40.573
A smaller or lower-scoring model can be better when it is stable

00:00:40.573 --> 00:00:43.130
and fast enough for the workflow.

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:46.964
Benchmark locally using representative images, prewritten rubrics, repeated runs,

00:00:46.964 --> 00:00:49.946
controlled settings, and a durable evaluation log.

00:00:49.946 --> 00:00:54.207
Community benchmarks provide hypotheses and hardware tiers, not permanent answers.

00:00:54.207 --> 00:00:58.467
Deployment adds privacy, licensing, retention, cost, power, monitoring, fallback, updates,

00:00:58.467 --> 00:00:59.745
and human review.

00:00:59.745 --> 00:01:02.727
Keep important decisions grounded in visible evidence.

00:01:02.727 --> 00:01:07.840
The best model is the configuration that meets task quality and reliability

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:12.100
on available hardware while preserving privacy and accountable human judgment.
